  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nRecent objective analysis and satellite imagery shows a weak\nshortwave and associated cloud cover moving into central and\neastern Iowa. Despite very dry air near the surface, this system\nwas able to squeeze out just enough moisture for some areas\nnorth of I-80, including Eppley Airfield, to record measurable\nrainfall amounts this afternoon (even if only 0.01\" was recorded\nthere officially). Northwesterly flow remains in place for much\nof the western CONUS, with cloud cover streaming into western \nNebraska ahead of a developing upper-level trough over the \nnorthern Rockies.\n\nThis next upper-level trough will continue to deepen as it \napproaches the Mid-Missouri River Valley. Being that it\noriginated over the Pacific Ocean, this trough will have more \nmoisture with which to work than today's system. Additionally, \nahead of the advancing trough, a band of enhanced 850-700-mb \nfrontogensis is forecast to develop, which will provide enhanced\nlift roughly along and just north of I-80 by Saturday \nafternoon. All of these ingredients will likely yield widespread\nrain beginning Saturday afternoon over our western counties, \nspreading south and east into southeastern Nebraska and \nsouthwestern Iowa by Saturday evening. Rainfall rates are not \nexpected to be overly heavy with system, but some locations in \nthe middle of our CWA, particularly along the Platte River, may \nhave rainfall totals exceeding half an inch. Rainfall will end \nfrom northwest to southeast across the CWA overnight Saturday \ninto Sunday, with all areas rain-free by sunrise Sunday as the \nupper-level trough pushes east of our area. While previous \nforecast cycles suggested there was a slight chance for a few \nsnowflakes to mix in as rain ends early Sunday, this forecast \nhas removed those chances entirely, as it looks like the \nbackside of the rain area should be able to stay ahead of \ntemperatures cold enough to yield any sort of frozen \nprecipitation. However, given that temperatures could drop \nbelow freezing briefly early on Sunday morning over \ncentral/northeast Nebraska, some slick spots may develop on \nroadways and elevated surfaces for a few hours around sunrise.\n\nAs the upper-level trough departs Sunday morning, strong\nnorthwesterly flow will develop at the surface as a polar\nairmass moves into Nebraska and Iowa. This will make for a\nfairly brisk day Sunday, with high temperatures only rising into\nthe low-40s, with sustained winds of 10-20 MPH and gusts up to\n30 MPH. \n\nFrom Monday through Wednesday next week, flow aloft will \ngradually become more southwesterly. Surface flow will also \nbecome much more southerly over time. This should lead to a \ngeneral warming trend in afternoon temperatures from mid-40s\nMonday to mid- to upper-50s by Wednesday. Some locations in\nsoutheastern Nebraska may even make a run at 60F Wednesday.\n\nBy late next week, a strong upper-level trough will emerge from\nthe southwestern CONUS, setting its sights on the Central \nPlains and Mid-Mississippi River Valley. This system is \nforecast to have strong upper-level support, conducive for \nstrengthening of a surface low as it moves northeast. Thus, this\nsystem is likely to produce a large area of precipitation, but \nmodels still have a fair amount of disagreement regarding \ntiming and location of the system track. As of right now, \nhowever, the vast majority of EPS, GEFS, and AIFS ensemble \nmembers keep our CWA above freezing, so most, if not all, of the\nprecipitation from this system should fall as rain, although \nthere is a non-zero chance of a few snowflakes mixing in before\nthe precipitation ends, primarily in our northern counties.",
